---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "How fast can you turn around a bid package?",
    a: "The standard turnaround is two working days from the point we agree the basis of the estimate. Repeat work priced from a rate library we have already built for you is frequently faster. Tell us the return date before anything else and we will give you a straight yes or no on whether we can hit it — we would rather decline a package than miss a bid deadline, because a late bid scores zero rather than a lower number."
  },
  {
    q: "Are you going to price the same job for our competitor?",
    a: "No. One package, one bidder, first to instruct. If we are already pricing a package you send us, we tell you immediately and decline the work. This is written into our engagement terms rather than being a matter of goodwill, and we will sign your non-disclosure agreement before any drawings change hands."
  },
  {
    q: "Who actually does the estimating?",
    a: "A desk rather than a single person. Between us we have thirty-three years of construction and estimating experience, and every pack is reviewed and signed off by a career estimator before it is released. Alongside that, a team of digital colleagues working inside the Vyntworks platform handles the mechanical work — measuring the drawings, extracting schedules, building the trade sections and assembling the documents. They follow our written pricing methods rather than improvising, they work to set approval limits, and every action is logged. You get the capacity of software with the judgment of someone who has priced this kind of work for years."
  },
  {
    q: "What software do you use, and why does that matter to me?",
    a: "Vyntworks — an operations platform built for contractors, covering estimating, procurement and cost control in one connected system. It matters to you directly, because you get an account on it. When a pack is ready you are notified, and the estimate is waiting there for you to review, adjust and issue rather than arriving as a flat document you can only accept or query. It is also where the speed, the traceable take-off and the automated checks come from. There is no licence attached to the account we set up for you, and if your bid volume ever makes running the whole thing in-house the cheaper answer, we will tell you and help you move."
  },
  {
    q: "Do we have to use the software?",
    a: "No. Every client gets an account because it is the fastest way to hand over an estimate you can actually interrogate, but plenty of people never log in and simply work from the PDF and the emailed summary. If you would rather we made every change, two revisions are included in the fee and you can send the rest by email. The account costs you nothing either way and carries no licence or commitment."
  },
  {
    q: "Who can see our estimate in there?",
    a: "You and the people you invite from your own business, plus the estimator working on your package. Your documents and your rate library sit in a tenancy specific to your company and are never pooled with, or used to price work for, anybody else. If you stop working with us, the rate library and priced models are yours and are exportable on request."
  },
  {
    q: "How accurate is the estimate?",
    a: "As accurate as the information we are given and the rates we are working from, which is exactly why every pack states both. Every priced quantity is traceable to the measurement or document it came from, and the pack is machine-audited before release: nothing can be priced without a source, nothing can be excluded without a stated reason, and every document you send has to be used or explicitly waived. Where the drawings will not support a firm price we carry a stated allowance rather than a guess, and it appears in the estimate where you can see it rather than quietly falling out."
  },
  {
    q: "Whose rates do you price from?",
    a: "Yours, wherever you have them. Most contractors have rates scattered across old spreadsheets and one estimator's memory, so part of the first job is pulling those into a proper rate library — we can import your existing Excel estimates to do it. Where you have gaps we price from published rate data or go out to your supply chain for real prices, and we mark clearly which is which. The library belongs to you and is exportable at any time, including if you stop working with us."
  },
  {
    q: "Will our client know you were involved?",
    a: "Only if you tell them. Everything is produced on your letterhead in your format, including your own Word or Excel quote template if you have one. We never contact your client, we are not named in the documents, and the estimate is issued by you under your name. The work is white-labeled as standard rather than as an extra."
  },
  {
    q: "Our drawings are scans and mark-ups. Is that a problem?",
    a: "No. The take-off reads scanned and photographed drawings as well as native PDFs, and reads the sheet number, title and declared scale from each drawing's own title block. Poor documents widen the assumptions we have to state and can slow us down; they do not stop us. A phone photograph of a marked-up plan is a legitimate starting point."
  },
  {
    q: "What happens when the addenda land mid-bid?",
    a: "We re-price against the new revision and hand you a line-by-line difference — what was added, what was removed, what changed, and exactly what it did to your total. Revised drawings issued late in a bid period are one of the most common ways a bid comes apart, and reading two hundred pages again to find the changes is exactly the work you should not be doing at that point."
  },
  {
    q: "Do you price work in the United Kingdom as well as North America?",
    a: "Yes. Alturascope runs a UK operation alongside its US and Canadian coverage, and packages are priced in the conventions, contract forms and currency of wherever the job is. If your work is in Britain, our UK estimating page sets it out in the terms a UK contractor or quantity surveyor expects."
  }
];

const schema = JSON.stringify({
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Service",
  "name": "Construction Estimating and Bid Support",
  "description": "Outsourced construction estimating for contractors. Send the drawings and receive a priced, machine-audited, white-labeled bid under your own name — usually within two working days.",
  "provider": {
    "@type": "ProfessionalService",
    "name": "Alturascope",
    "url": "https://alturascope.com/",
    "areaServed": [
      { "@type": "Country", "name": "United States" },
      { "@type": "Country", "name": "Canada" },
      { "@type": "Country", "name": "United Kingdom" }
    ]
  },
  "serviceType": "Construction Estimating Service",
  "audience": {
    "@type": "BusinessAudience",
    "audienceType": "General contractors, subcontractors, specialist trade contractors and fit-out firms"
  },
  "hasOfferCatalog": {
    "@type": "OfferCatalog",
    "name": "Estimating Desk Services",
    "itemListElement": [
      { "@type": "Offer", "itemOffered": { "@type": "Service", "name": "Bid Package Estimating" } },
      { "@type": "Offer", "itemOffered": { "@type": "Service", "name": "Quantity Take-Off from Drawings" } },
      { "@type": "Offer", "itemOffered": { "@type": "Service", "name": "Rate Library Development" } },
      { "@type": "Offer", "itemOffered": { "@type": "Service", "name": "Subcontractor Bid Support" } },
      { "@type": "Offer", "itemOffered": { "@type": "Service", "name": "Supply Chain Enquiry and Bid Leveling" } }
    ]
  }
});

const breadcrumbSchema = JSON.stringify({
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  "itemListElement": [
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://alturascope.com/" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Estimating", "item": "https://alturascope.com/estimating/" }
  ]
});

const faqSchema = JSON.stringify({
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": faqs.map((f) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    "name": f.q,
    "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": f.a }
  }))
});
---
<Layout
  title="Construction Estimating & Bid Support Service | Alturascope"
  description="Send us the drawings and get back a priced, client-ready bid under your own name, usually in two working days. Machine-audited estimating for contractors."
  canonical="https://alturascope.com/estimating/"
  ogImage="https://alturascope.com/Images/plan-1.jpg"
  schema={schema}
  breadcrumbSchema={breadcrumbSchema}
  faqSchema={faqSchema}
  fullWidth={true}
  hreflang={[
    { lang: "en", href: "https://alturascope.com/estimating/" },
    { lang: "en-GB", href: "https://alturascope.com/uk/estimating/" },
    { lang: "x-default", href: "https://alturascope.com/estimating/" }
  ]}
>
  <div data-hero-page>

    <!-- Hero -->
    <section class="relative min-h-[78vh] md:min-h-0 md:h-[66vh] flex items-center justify-center">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 bg-navy">
        <img
          src="/Images/plan-1.jpg"
          alt="Construction bid drawings being read on a desk during a quantity take-off and estimate"
          class="w-full h-full object-cover opacity-35"
          loading="eager"
          fetchpriority="high"
          decoding="async"
          width="1920"
          height="1440"
        />
        <div class="absolute inset-0 bg-[rgba(11,31,58,0.72)]"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="relative z-10 text-center px-6 pt-28 pb-16 md:pt-0 md:pb-0 max-w-[920px] mx-auto">
        <p class="label text-gold mb-4">The Estimating Desk</p>
        <h1 class="text-[1.8rem] md:text-[3.2rem] font-light text-offwhite leading-tight">
          Construction Estimating and Bid Support, Under Your Own Name
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-6 text-offwhite/80 text-[1.05rem] md:text-[1.2rem] leading-body max-w-[680px] mx-auto">
          Send us the drawings. We&rsquo;ll send back a priced, client-ready bid &mdash; machine-audited, white-labeled, and usually inside two working days.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#send-a-package" class="btn-primary">Send Us a Live Package</a>
          <a href="/estimating/basis-of-estimate/" class="btn-secondary-light">See What&rsquo;s On Every Pack</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- In-page navigation -->
    <nav class="sticky top-[84px] z-40 bg-white/95 backdrop-blur border-b border-border" aria-label="On this page">
      <div class="max-w-[1200px] mx-auto px-6">
        <ul class="flex items-center gap-6 md:gap-9 overflow-x-auto whitespace-nowrap py-3.5 -mb-px list-none m-0">
          <li><a href="#what-you-get" class="subnav-link text-[0.82rem] font-medium tracking-nav text-midgrey hover:text-navy transition-colors">What you get</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-audit" class="subnav-link text-[0.82rem] font-medium tracking-nav text-midgrey hover:text-navy transition-colors">The audit</a></li>
          <li><a href="#how-it-works" class="subnav-link text-[0.82rem] font-medium tracking-nav text-midgrey hover:text-navy transition-colors">How it works</a></li>
          <li><a href="#pricing" class="subnav-link text-[0.82rem] font-medium tracking-nav text-midgrey hover:text-navy transition-colors">Pricing</a></li>
          <li><a href="#faq" class="subnav-link text-[0.82rem] font-medium tracking-nav text-midgrey hover:text-navy transition-colors">Questions</a></li>
          <li class="ml-auto pl-6 hidden md:block"><a href="#send-a-package" class="text-[0.82rem] font-medium tracking-nav text-navy border-b-2 border-gold pb-0.5">Send a bid package</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </nav>

    <!-- Pain mirror -->
    <section class="bg-navy-light section-padding">
      <div class="max-w-[1100px] mx-auto px-6">
        <p class="label text-gold mb-10 text-center">Sound Familiar?</p>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 md:gap-x-12">
          <p class="text-offwhite text-[1.1rem] font-light leading-snug border-l-2 border-gold pl-5">
            &ldquo;The package is back Friday. Our estimator&rsquo;s on vacation.&rdquo;
          </p>
          <p class="text-offwhite text-[1.1rem] font-light leading-snug border-l-2 border-gold pl-5">
            &ldquo;We turned down four invitations last month. Not because we didn&rsquo;t want them &mdash; nobody had time to price them.&rdquo;
          </p>
          <p class="text-offwhite text-[1.1rem] font-light leading-snug border-l-2 border-gold pl-5">
            &ldquo;I priced it at the kitchen table on Sunday night.&rdquo;
          </p>
          <p class="text-offwhite text-[1.1rem] font-light leading-snug border-l-2 border-gold pl-5">
            &ldquo;We won it. Then found the firestopping wasn&rsquo;t in it.&rdquo;
          </p>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-12 text-center text-offwhite/75 leading-body max-w-[56ch] mx-auto">
          None of those are estimating problems. They are revenue problems that happen to look like workload.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Stats -->
    <section class="bg-offwhite py-12 md:py-[48px]">
      <div class="max-w-[1100px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-8 md:gap-4 text-center">
          <div>
            <p class="text-[2.4rem] font-light text-navy">33</p>
            <p class="text-[0.7rem] font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-midgrey mt-1">Years Construction &amp; Estimating</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="text-[2.4rem] font-light text-navy">2 Days</p>
            <p class="text-[0.7rem] font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-midgrey mt-1">Typical Turnaround</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="text-[2.4rem] font-light text-navy">Your Name</p>
            <p class="text-[0.7rem] font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-midgrey mt-1">White-Labeled As Standard</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="text-[2.4rem] font-light text-navy">$40M+</p>
            <p class="text-[0.7rem] font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-midgrey mt-1">Largest Single Project Priced</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- See the pack -->
    <section class="bg-white section-padding">
      <div class="max-w-[1100px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-2 gap-12 lg:gap-16 items-center">
          <div>
            <p class="label text-gold mb-4">Before You Send Anything</p>
            <h2 class="text-[2rem] font-light text-navy leading-tight">
              Read a complete pack first.
            </h2>
            <p class="mt-6 text-midgrey leading-body">
              We have published a full demonstration estimate pack &mdash; all eleven pages, both
              a US bid and a UK tender &mdash; so you can judge the work before there is anything
              of yours at stake. Priced schedule, traceable take-off, margin analysis and the
              audit report, nothing held back for the sales call.
            </p>
            <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-4">
              <a href="/estimating/sample-quote-pack/" class="btn-primary">See every page</a>
              <a href="/downloads/alturascope-demo-estimate-pack-us.pdf" download class="btn-secondary">Download the PDF</a>
            </div>
          </div>
          <figure class="m-0">
            <img
              src="/Images/pack/us-03.jpg"
              alt="Priced schedule of works from an Alturascope estimate pack, showing 23 trade sections with net cost, gross profit and price"
              width="1400"
              height="989"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
              class="w-full h-auto border border-border"
            />
            <figcaption class="mt-3 text-midgrey text-[0.8rem]">
              Page 3 &mdash; the priced schedule. Demonstration figures.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- The problem -->
    <section class="bg-white section-padding">
      <div class="max-w-[900px] mx-auto px-6">
        <p class="label text-gold mb-4">The Problem</p>
        <h2 class="text-[2rem] font-light text-navy leading-tight">
          Estimating capacity is a ceiling on revenue.
        </h2>
        <div class="mt-6 space-y-4 text-midgrey leading-body">
          <p>
            Every contractor knows how many bids they submitted last quarter. Very few know how many invitations they declined because nobody had the hours to price them. That second number is the more important one, because each of those was a job somebody wanted you to win and you chose not to compete for.
          </p>
          <p>
            The constraint is rarely willingness and it is almost never capability. It is that estimating is bursty and salaries are not. Demand arrives in clusters &mdash; four packages land in the same week, all returnable the same Friday &mdash; and a permanent estimator sized for the peak is idle for most of the year, while one sized for the average turns work away every time the peak arrives. Estimators are also scarce, expensive to recruit, and they carry the method in their heads, which means the day one leaves is the day bidding stops.
          </p>
          <p>
            The other half of the problem is what happens after you win. Bids are lost on price, but margin is lost on omissions: the firestopping that was never in the schedule, the quantity that came from a scaled drawing nobody checked, the exclusion somebody made at eleven at night and forgot to write down. A cheap estimate that is wrong is the most expensive document in the business.
          </p>
          <p>
            An estimating desk you can borrow solves the first problem. Doing it inside a system that will not let an unsourced quantity or an unstated exclusion through solves the second. That combination is what this service is.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- What comes back -->
    <section id="what-you-get" class="scroll-mt-[150px] bg-offwhite section-padding">
      <div class="max-w-[1180px] mx-auto px-6">
        <p class="label text-gold mb-4">What Comes Back</p>
        <h2 class="text-[2rem] font-light text-navy leading-tight">
          Not a number. A pack you can defend.
        </h2>
        <div class="mt-6 space-y-4 text-midgrey leading-body">
          <p>
            Anyone can send you a total. What wins work &mdash; and what protects the margin after you have won it &mdash; is being able to show exactly how the total was built, exactly what it assumes, and exactly which measurement on which drawing every quantity came from. Every package we price returns the same six things.
          </p>
        </div>






        <div class="mt-14 grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-x-14 gap-y-12">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-[1.35rem] font-medium text-navy">Priced Schedule of Works</h3>
          <div class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-midgrey leading-body">
            <p>
              Every trade section, every line, with quantities, rates and gross profit set out so you can interrogate them &mdash; not a black box with a figure at the bottom. The structure follows the way your business already prices, and where you use a schedule of values or a defined breakdown for the client, the estimate is built to produce it directly rather than being reformatted afterwards.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-[1.35rem] font-medium text-navy">Traceable Quantity Take-Off</h3>
          <div class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-midgrey leading-body">
            <p>
              Quantities are not typed in. They are measured on the drawing and linked to the line they price, so every figure walks back to the shape it came from on the sheet it came from. We can hand you the drawing rendered with the take-off drawn over it &mdash; area fills, run centerlines, count markers &mdash; so you can look at what was measured rather than take a number on trust. When your client questions a quantity, the answer is a picture rather than an argument.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-[1.35rem] font-medium text-navy">Margin Analysis</h3>
          <div class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-midgrey leading-body">
            <p>
              Gross profit by section and overall, so you can see where the job makes money before you decide what to submit and where you have room to sharpen. Most outsourced estimating stops at the number, which leaves you asking &ldquo;is this right?&rdquo; A margin breakdown changes the question to &ldquo;where do I compete?&rdquo; &mdash; which is a commercial conversation, and a considerably better one to be having the night before a bid goes in.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-[1.35rem] font-medium text-navy">A Stated Basis of Estimate</h3>
          <div class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-midgrey leading-body">
            <p>
              Every drawing and specification priced, listed by number and revision, together with inclusions, exclusions, assumptions, allowances and qualifications. It is written to be attached to your submission as it stands, so the scope you priced is the scope on the record. Our <a href="/estimating/basis-of-estimate/" class="text-navy font-medium hover:opacity-80 transition-opacity">Basis of Estimate standard</a> sets out exactly what appears on every pack and why.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-[1.35rem] font-medium text-navy">A Client-Ready Bid Document</h3>
          <div class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-midgrey leading-body">
            <p>
              Your letterhead, your terms, your covering narrative &mdash; generated from the live numbers rather than typed up afterwards, so the document cannot disagree with the estimate behind it. If you already have a Word or Excel quote template your clients recognize, we produce the bid in yours rather than ours. Presentation moves win rates on price-close bids, and a small firm should not lose one because the submission looked like a spreadsheet next to a competitor's brochure.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-[1.35rem] font-medium text-navy">The Working File &mdash; And It Is Yours</h3>
          <div class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-midgrey leading-body">
            <p>
              The priced model itself, exportable, together with the rate library we build for you along the way. That library is the real asset: it captures how your business prices, it draws on what materials and subcontracts have actually cost rather than what a price book says they should, and it gets sharper with every job we do. It belongs to you. If you stop working with us tomorrow, you take it with you.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Machine audit -->
    <section id="the-audit" class="scroll-mt-[150px] bg-navy section-padding">
      <div class="max-w-[1000px] mx-auto px-6">
        <p class="label text-gold mb-4">Before You See It</p>
        <h2 class="text-[2rem] font-light text-offwhite leading-tight max-w-[24ch]">
          Every pack is machine-audited. It cannot be released while anything is red.
        </h2>
        <div class="mt-6 space-y-4 text-offwhite/70 leading-body max-w-[68ch]">
          <p>
            Estimates rarely fail because somebody applied a rate wrongly. They fail because a drawing went unread, a quantity had no source, or something was quietly dropped and nobody noticed until the job was won and the scope was fixed.
          </p>
          <p>
            So before any pack leaves the desk, the platform runs a fixed set of automated checks across the estimate, the take-off and every document we were sent. They are deterministic rather than discretionary &mdash; not a reviewer's good intentions, but rules enforced by the system &mdash; and the job cannot be marked complete while any of them is failing. The audit report is issued with the pack, so you can see what was verified.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-14 grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 lg:gap-x-14">
          <div class="border-t border-white/15 py-5">
            <p class="label text-gold mb-2">Provenance</p>
            <p class="text-offwhite/80 leading-body"><span class="text-offwhite font-medium">Every priced quantity has a source.</span> A line that is neither linked to a measurement nor cited to a document is flagged. There is no such thing as a number that came from nowhere.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border-t border-white/15 py-5">
            <p class="label text-gold mb-2">No Silent Drops</p>
            <p class="text-offwhite/80 leading-body"><span class="text-offwhite font-medium">Nothing can be excluded without a stated reason.</span> And &ldquo;we couldn&rsquo;t measure it&rdquo; is not an accepted reason &mdash; unmeasurable scope has to appear in the estimate at zero, marked to be confirmed, where you can see it. This is the check that stops the firestopping disappearing.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border-t border-white/15 py-5">
            <p class="label text-gold mb-2">Nothing Unread</p>
            <p class="text-offwhite/80 leading-body"><span class="text-offwhite font-medium">Every document you send has to be used or waived.</span> If a drawing or specification we were given is not cited by a single quantity or decision, it is raised before the pack goes out rather than discovered afterwards.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border-t border-white/15 py-5">
            <p class="label text-gold mb-2">Measure First</p>
            <p class="text-offwhite/80 leading-body"><span class="text-offwhite font-medium">Prices cannot exist before measurements do.</span> The take-off has to be the basis of the price, not a justification assembled after the number was reached.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border-t border-white/15 py-5">
            <p class="label text-gold mb-2">Arithmetic Sanity</p>
            <p class="text-offwhite/80 leading-body"><span class="text-offwhite font-medium">Totals are checked against the drawings&rsquo; own figures.</span> Where the measured areas and the areas printed on the sheets disagree by more than a set margin, the pack stops and somebody looks at it.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border-t border-white/15 py-5">
            <p class="label text-gold mb-2">No Double-Counts</p>
            <p class="text-offwhite/80 leading-body"><span class="text-offwhite font-medium">The same space drawn twice is caught.</span> Before anything is measured the system reads the drawing register and compares sheets geometrically, so a site plan and a floor plan showing the same footprint at different scales are never measured as two separate floors.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border-t border-white/15 py-5">
            <p class="label text-gold mb-2">Units</p>
            <p class="text-offwhite/80 leading-body"><span class="text-offwhite font-medium">Dimensions have to agree.</span> A linear measurement cannot quietly feed an area rate. Conversions are permitted, but only where they are declared as an assumption you can read.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border-t border-white/15 py-5">
            <p class="label text-gold mb-2">Drift</p>
            <p class="text-offwhite/80 leading-body"><span class="text-offwhite font-medium">Linked measurements are re-checked at the end.</span> If a measurement changed after a line was priced from it, the mismatch surfaces rather than silently persisting into your submission.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="mt-10 text-offwhite/50 text-[0.9rem] leading-body max-w-[70ch]">
